<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philae</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philae is an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> near Aswan, at the southern frontier of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, famed for its temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>, begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-i">Nectanebo I</a> and built mainly under the Ptolemies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. The sanctuary drew worshippers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, including the Blemmyes, until its closure under Justinian around 537. When the Aswan dams flooded the island, its temples were moved in the 1970s to the nearby island of Agilkia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philae.jpg" alt="Philae" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Holger Uwe Schmitt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1977_bis_1980_wurde_die_Tempel_auf_der_Insel_Philae_auf_die_h%C3%B6her_gelegene_Insel_Agilka_versetzt._01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
